# eyeball's configuration, installed to /etc/default/eyeball.
#
# The deploy copies this file only when the Pi does not already have one, so a fresh card comes
# up configured and a file tuned on site is never overwritten. That makes this the reference
# copy and the Pi's the operative one: edit the Pi's during a session, then bring the changes
# back here so the next card starts where this one left off.
#
# It holds a password, so the deploy installs it 0600 and it is the reason no camera settings
# live in the repository's Python. Change the password here and this file stops being safe to
# commit — keep the placeholder, and set the real one on the Pi.

EYEBALL_CAMERA_HOST=192.168.0.90
EYEBALL_CAMERA_USER=root
EYEBALL_CAMERA_PASSWORD=changeme

# The box the mage stands in, as fractions of the frame: x,y,w,h. The region the estimator runs
# on and the anchor everything else is judged against. Square is worth arranging — the model takes
# a square input, and a crop that is not one arrives letterboxed with the margins spending real
# input resolution on black. 0.5625 of a 16:9 frame is exactly its own height.
EYEBALL_CROP=0.21875,0,0.5625,1

EYEBALL_CAMERA_RESOLUTION=480x270

# How the picture is fetched. `rtsp` is H.264 and `mjpeg` is independent JPEGs over HTTP; the
# Axis serves either at any resolution, so this is a free choice and the reasoning is in
# EYEBALL.md §4.
#
# It is also the first thing to try when the preview runs late. H.264 carries latency this rig
# cannot see into — an RTSP jitter buffer, and a GOP structure that means a frame cannot be
# decoded until its references have been — while MJPEG has no inter-frame state, no GOP delay
# and no buffer to tune. The bandwidth it costs instead is irrelevant on a dedicated link with
# one camera on it. Switching costs one word here and a restart, so measure rather than argue —
# and measure latency by filming a running clock, not with a rate off the broker. EYEBALL.md
# §4.1 is why that is the method and why no rate can stand in for it.
#
# Commented because this is the code's own default. Uncomment only to overrule it.
#EYEBALL_CAMERA_TRANSPORT=mjpeg

# What the camera is asked to send, which has to sit a little above what the estimator achieves.
# Below it and the stream becomes the ceiling — the loop runs at the camera's rate and no amount
# of tuning downstream shows any effect at all, which is exactly what a request of 10 did once
# pose reached 10. Far above it and the capture thread decodes frames that are dropped unread,
# on the same three cores the model is using.
#
# eyeball/status/captured_fps against eyeball/status/fps is the readout: the first should stay a
# little ahead of the second, and the gap between them is decode being thrown away.
#
# Neither says anything about how old a frame is, and no rate ever can — a pipeline running three
# seconds late still reports the rate it was asked for. What captured_fps does catch is the one
# delay that grows: decode slower than the camera delivers drags it below the number above.
#
# Age is eyeball/status/sighting_age_ms, and preview_age_ms beside it. The first is the rig's own
# latency and the one to tune against; the second adds a drawing and an encode the show never
# waits for. EYEBALL.md §4.1 has what to subtract them from.
# Pi 3B can do ~15 fps
EYEBALL_CAMERA_FPS=15

# What an arm segment has to look like before its angle is believed: how sure the model is of a
# joint, and how long the segment looks against the body it belongs to. Both are held wide on
# purpose — a coarse angle still moving the right way is worth more to a child than a number that
# blanks — and both are numbers a kid in front of the camera is expected to move.
#
# The confidence one is the arms' own and is deliberately below EYEBALL_MIN_CONFIDENCE, which
# decides what gets drawn and whether there is a mage at all. Loosen this one, not that one.
#
# Commented because these are the code's own defaults. Uncomment only to overrule them.
#EYEBALL_ARM_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.20
#EYEBALL_ARM_MIN_LENGTH=0.20

# How the crop is shrunk to the model's input, and left to the daemon on purpose. Bilinear is
# right while the crop is near the model's input size, area-averaging is right if it is ever
# widened enough for the downscale to get steep — so the correct value follows the crop above,
# and the default already knows it. Uncomment only to overrule that, never to restate it.
#EYEBALL_RESIZE=area

# What the camera is forced to on every start of the daemon, comma-separated key=value, in VAPIX
# parameter names. Each is read back afterwards and any disagreement is logged by name and
# published under eyeball/camera/requested/ beside what the camera says it actually is.
#
# A setting this firmware refuses reappears as a mismatch on every start until it is deleted from
# here. MaxSlowShutter was one such on the M3105-LVE and has been deleted: 6.15.2 answers
# `Error setting ... to '10000'` and the read-back still says 33333, so it is not refused-but-
# maybe, it is unsettable. Which of the two exposure ceilings binds the sensor stopped being a
# question worth carrying a line for once only one of them could be written at all.
#
# The list below is the night configuration, which is the one the show runs. Infrared beats
# visible light on this rig by a wide margin, so there is no day list any more; the reasoning is
# in EYEBALL.md §5.
#
#   IrCutFilter=no          the sensor sees 850 nm, lit by the camera's own illuminator
#   WhiteBalance            locked, so a wash with no green in it cannot be chased
#   Exposure=auto           bounded rather than locked, by the ceiling below
#   CaptureFrequency=50Hz   the mains here; 60Hz buys banding and a wrong quantisation
#   MaxExposureTime=10000   10 ms, the shortest a 50 Hz flicker quantisation offers, and the
#                           difference between a landmark on a moving wrist and a streak. The
#                           camera's own default is 2000000 — two seconds — which is not a
#                           blurred landmark but half a frame per second, and looks from the
#                           outside exactly like a preview running seconds behind. This one does
#                           take on 6.15.2, confirmed in the start-up log, which is what rules
#                           the exposure out as a cause of lag rather than assuming it away.
#   ExposurePriority=0      spend light on a short shutter rather than on low noise: a pose
#                           model tolerates noise far better than it tolerates blur
#
#   Rotation=180            the camera is mounted inverted, and this is what puts the picture
#                           back up the right way. It was set by hand in the web UI and nothing
#                           reproduced it: a factory reset or a replacement camera would come up
#                           with an upside-down picture, MoveNet would find nobody, and the rig
#                           would look broken for a reason nothing in this file mentioned. The
#                           crop above is symmetric about the centre and full height, so a 180°
#                           rotation maps it onto itself and it needs no change alongside this.
#                           It is also the one Image-group setting here — see below.
#
# Bounded is not locked, and the show now moves the thing the bound is metering. The heads stand
# beside the camera and light the mage from the camera's own viewpoint, and the movement envelope
# modulates that light with how much the child is moving — so an auto exposure, however tightly
# ceilinged, re-meters against the show and the landmarks shift with the brightness rather than
# with the person. ImageSource.I0.Sensor.Exposure=hold is the lock, and it is left commented
# because `hold` freezes the sensor at whatever it was metering the instant the daemon started:
# on a rig whose beams come up afterwards, that is a lock onto a dark field. It wants setting
# with the show already lit, and confirming in the start-up log like everything else here.
#
# Not the IR illuminator, whatever its name suggests. Layout.IRIlluminationEnabled reads `yes`
# here and is a web UI toggle rather than the lamp — it sits among HeaterEnabled, SnapshotEnabled
# and ShowAMCToolbar, and this model has no root.Light group at all. IrCutFilter above is the only
# infrared control there is; do not add the Layout one thinking it does something.
#
# Two groups are settable and only the rotation is taken from the second. The rest of the
# camera's `Image` group, which holds the whole H.264 encoder, is left to whatever the camera
# decided: a 32-frame GOP, main profile, unlimited VBR. That is the same "decides for
# itself, badly" this variable exists to close, and it is where H.264's latency lives. The
# parameters, their values and what each one buys are in EYEBALL.md §4.2, ready to paste — left
# out of the line below on purpose, because forcing an encoder before measuring whether the
# delay is even upstream is arguing rather than measuring.
#
# The clock overlay from EYEBALL.md §4.1 belongs in that same group, and is the one thing here
# that measures rather than changes anything:
#
#   Image.I0.Text.TextEnabled=yes,Image.I0.Text.ClockEnabled=yes,Image.I0.Text.DateEnabled=yes
#
# Read §4.1 before trusting what it shows: this camera has no NTP server it can reach, so the
# overlay carries an unsynchronised clock and the difference against the Pi's is an offset plus
# a delay. Filming a clock has no such problem and needs nothing set here at all.
#
EYEBALL_CAMERA_SETTINGS=ImageSource.I0.DayNight.IrCutFilter=no,ImageSource.I0.Sensor.WhiteBalance=fixed_outdoor1,ImageSource.I0.Sensor.Exposure=auto,ImageSource.I0.CaptureFrequency=50Hz,ImageSource.I0.Sensor.MaxExposureTime=10000,ImageSource.I0.Sensor.ExposurePriority=0,ImageSource.I0.Sensor.ExposurePriorityLowlight=0,Image.I0.Appearance.Rotation=180
